          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which lymph node characteristic on palpation is most suggestive of lymphoma?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which lymph node characteristic on palpation is most suggestive of lymphoma?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hard and fixed to underlying tissue</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A hard, fixed node indicates infiltration into surrounding structures — the hallmark of metastatic carcinoma, not lymphoma.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Soft, tender, and fluctuant</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Soft, tender, fluctuant nodes indicate acute pyogenic infection with possible abscess formation, not a neoplastic process.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Firm and rubbery with preserved mobility</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Lymphoma characteristically produces firm, rubbery nodes that remain mobile because the capsule is intact. This consistency reflects the homogeneous proliferation of lymphoid cells.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Matted with central softening</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Matted nodes with central softening point to tuberculous lymphadenitis, where caseation necrosis causes softening and periadenitis fuses adjacent nodes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the six bedside discriminators: distribution, tenderness, consistency, mobility, size progression, and site. Each consistency pattern maps to a specific aetiology."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Lymphoma produces firm, rubbery, mobile nodes — one of the six bedside discriminators that help narrow the differential diagnosis of lymphadenopathy."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
                "type": "auto",
