        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 340.5,
            "to": 350.5
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In tuberculous lymphadenitis, which cytokine released by CD4-positive Th1 cells </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In tuberculous lymphadenitis, which cytokine released by CD4-positive Th1 cells is primarily responsible for transforming macrophages into epithelioid histiocytes?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Interleukin-4</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Interleukin-4 is a Th2 cytokine that drives humoral immunity and IgE class-switching. Tuberculous granuloma formation is a Th1-mediated, cell-mediated process.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Tumour necrosis factor-alpha</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>TNF-α contributes to caseous necrosis by driving macrophage apoptosis, but it is not the cytokine responsible for epithelioid transformation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Interleukin-10</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Interleukin-10 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine that suppresses macrophage activation — the opposite of what granuloma formation requires.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Interferon-gamma</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. IFN-γ is the master activating cytokine in Type IV delayed hypersensitivity. It converts macrophages into epithelioid histiocytes, which then fuse to form Langhans giant cells.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Recall the cascade: mycobacterial antigens are presented via MHC class II to CD4+ Th1 cells, which release IFN-γ. This master cytokine activates macrophages into epithelioid histiocytes."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "IFN-γ released by Th1 cells is the key driver of epithelioid transformation that builds the tuberculous granuloma — a classic Type IV delayed hypersensitivity response."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
