          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 25-year-old man presents with a painless, rubbery, 3 cm cervical lymph node pe</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 25-year-old man presents with a painless, rubbery, 3 cm cervical lymph node persisting for eight weeks. FNAC shows a mixed population of lymphocytes but is non-diagnostic. What is the most appropriate next step?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Reassure the patient and review after three months</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>An unexplained node persisting beyond six weeks demands tissue diagnosis. Delayed biopsy risks missing a curable early-stage lymphoma.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Perform an excision biopsy to preserve nodal architecture</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Excision biopsy is mandatory when lymphoma is suspected because FNAC cannot demonstrate the growth pattern (follicular versus diffuse) needed for subtyping. The tissue is triaged for morphology, flow cytometry, and cytogenetics.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Start empirical anti-tubercular therapy</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Empirical ATT without tissue confirmation risks masking an underlying lymphoma. A painless, rubbery, mobile node is more suggestive of lymphoma than tuberculosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Order a PET-CT scan as the definitive investigation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>PET-CT is used for staging after histological diagnosis is established. It cannot substitute for tissue biopsy, which alone reveals the lymphoma subtype.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "When lymphoma is suspected, FNAC is insufficient because it reads individual cells but cannot preserve the growth pattern. Excision biopsy is the mandatory next step for subtyping."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excision biopsy is the gold standard when lymphoma is suspected — it preserves nodal architecture and enables IHC, flow cytometry, and cytogenetics, none of which FNAC can provide."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
